From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: explorer: an elevated program's windows have taskbar buttons

An elevated program (ADR 0012) runs as SYSTEM on an X display of its own,
which sg-compositor composites into the user's desktop; no program in the
session can type into it or read it (bug B56). Its windows are therefore not
on the session's Wine desktop, and the shell hook never reports them: without
this the taskbar showed no button for an installer or an elevated Control
Panel, and a minimised one could not be brought back.

sg-elevated-run names the elevated program's Wine desktop after its display:
WinSta0\sg-elevated-<display>. Once a second the taskbar enumerates those
desktops' top-level windows and gives each a button of its own group, drawn
like any other; its title is read from the server (InternalGetWindowText),
not by sending the window a message. A click asks the compositor to bring the
window forward -- sg-lockctl ACTIVATE <display> <X window> on the session's
control socket, which is focus, not input -- rather than messaging the
elevated program, and there is no window menu for it: the session does not
drive an elevated program (UIPI; wine-sg 0291).

Forwarded: not-needed
